WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.220
The worked island refilled on paper, toward a steady count, while species kept

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.500
coming and going. So empty a real island of every insect and spider.

3
00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:17.520
How many species come back, and are they the same ones?

4
00:00:18.583 --> 00:00:23.343
Most islands were colonised long ago, so nobody saw their rates from the start.

5
00:00:25.023 --> 00:00:29.643
Daniel Simberloff and Edward Wilson chose islands small enough to empty, and then

6
00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:30.643
watched them fill.

7
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:35.437
They used six small islands of red mangrove in the Florida Keys.

8
00:00:36.837 --> 00:00:42.337
Each carried a community of terrestrial arthropods: insects, spiders, mites and

9
00:00:42.337 --> 00:00:43.337
their relatives.

10
00:00:44.017 --> 00:00:47.877
First they recorded every arthropod species on each island.

11
00:00:49.157 --> 00:00:52.357
Then they covered each island with a tent and fumigated it.

12
00:00:53.517 --> 00:00:56.877
Afterwards they censused the islands again and again for a year.

13
00:00:57.967 --> 00:00:59.427
The count climbed back.

14
00:01:00.687 --> 00:01:06.587
By about day 250, most islands held about as many species as untreated islands.

15
00:01:07.907 --> 00:01:10.287
The exception was E1, the most distant.

16
00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:14.687
Population densities, though, were still abnormally low.

17
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.250
Now the second question.

18
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.770
The count came back.

19
00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.390
How much of the old species list do you expect the new list to share?

20
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:35.667
Take three mite kinds from island E9, chosen because their records are clear.

21
00:01:37.087 --> 00:01:38.507
One was there before and after.

22
00:01:39.727 --> 00:01:41.167
One was there only before.

23
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:43.787
One was there only after.

24
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.920
Two kinds before, and two kinds after, so the count is unchanged.

25
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.900
But only one of the three is shared.

26
00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.000
The Jaccard similarity is shared species divided by species in either list.

27
00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:00.440
Here it is 1/3.

28
00:02:01.483 --> 00:02:02.643
Read it with care.

29
00:02:04.003 --> 00:02:07.023
These three are a chosen subset, not the whole community.

30
00:02:08.423 --> 00:02:12.403
And finding the shared mite at both censuses does not mean it survived.

31
00:02:13.723 --> 00:02:16.303
It was recorded again after recolonisation.

32
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:21.427
A second report followed four islands to the end of the second year.

33
00:02:22.827 --> 00:02:27.247
Species numbers had changed little from the year before, while immigrations and

34
00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:29.527
extinctions continued at high rates.

35
00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:35.777
A steady count with continuing replacement is what the model calls a dynamic

36
00:02:35.777 --> 00:02:41.497
equilibrium. The result fits the model, though on its own it does not prove the

37
00:02:41.497 --> 00:02:42.497
model's mechanism.

38
00:02:43.417 --> 00:02:45.337
And the rates themselves were rough.

39
00:02:46.697 --> 00:02:51.917
Censuses came at intervals, so some arrivals and losses fell between visits and were

40
00:02:51.917 --> 00:02:58.177
never seen. And a resident overlooked at one census looked like a loss and a return.
